# Task Tracker - App Flow

## 1. App Launch

1. `main.dart` initializes:
   - Firebase
   - Hive + secure storage + shared preferences
   - local notifications
   - theme and localization providers
2. Splash screen decides the first route.

## 2. Splash Decision Flow

The splash screen checks:
- Is onboarding completed?
- Is the user logged in?
- Is the email verified?

Navigation result:
- First launch -> Onboarding
- Onboarding completed + logged out -> Login
- Logged in but email not verified -> Email Verification
- Logged in + verified -> Home Dashboard

## 3. Onboarding Flow

The onboarding sequence introduces:
- task organization
- reminders and deadlines
- progress tracking

When onboarding finishes, a persistent flag is saved and the user is sent to Login.

## 4. Authentication Flow

Available screens:
- Login
- Sign Up
- Forgot Password
- Email Verification

Supported behaviors:
- email/password sign in
- email/password sign up
- remember me
- reset password email
- logout

After successful authentication and verification, the app opens the Home Dashboard.

## 5. Home Dashboard Flow

The dashboard is the main shell of the app. It provides four sections:
- Tasks
- Profile
- Leaderboard
- Settings

The dashboard keeps navigation in one place and adapts to phone and tablet layouts.

## 6. Task Flow

Task screen responsibilities:
- load all user tasks
- sync local Hive data with Firestore
- show filtered task lists
- sort tasks by due date, created date, or priority

Task creation:
- user enters title
- optional description
- due date / end time
- priority
- category
- optional reminder time

Task validation:
- reminder cannot be in the past
- reminder must be before the task end time
- reminder must be at least 10 minutes before end time

Task actions:
- create task
- update task
- mark complete / incomplete
- delete task
- delete all tasks from settings

Notification behavior tied to tasks:
- a reminder notification can be scheduled at `reminderAt`
- an end-of-timeframe notification can also be scheduled at task end time
- completed or deleted tasks cancel their scheduled notifications

## 7. Notification Flow

The app uses local notifications for task reminders.

Initialization:
- notification permission is requested on app startup
- on Android, exact alarm capability is also requested when needed

Scheduling rules:
- reminders are only scheduled if the global Task Reminders setting is enabled
- reminders use timezone-aware scheduling
- Android uses `exactAllowWhileIdle` to improve delivery during idle modes

Global toggle behavior:
- OFF: all pending reminder notifications are cancelled
- ON: active incomplete tasks with valid future reminders are scheduled again

Overdue notifications:
- on app initialization, the app checks overdue incomplete tasks
- a general overdue notification is shown at most once per day

## 8. Battery Optimization Flow

Android battery optimization can delay or suppress reminders, especially on aggressive OEM devices.

Settings behavior:
- the Settings screen shows a Battery Optimization row under Task Reminders
- the row reflects whether the app is currently exempt from optimization
- tapping the control opens the Android system battery optimization screen

Important platform note:
- the app cannot silently force this setting on or off
- Android requires the user to confirm the battery optimization change in system UI

## 9. Profile Flow

Profile features:
- create or update profile details
- manage avatar/photo
- view task statistics
- view streak values

The profile area focuses on user identity and progress.

## 10. Leaderboard Flow

Leaderboard features:
- fetch users from repository/provider
- display ranking data
- support progressive loading of more entries

## 11. Settings Flow

Current settings include:
- theme: system / light / dark
- language: English / Hindi
- task reminders: enable / disable
- battery optimization shortcut on Android
- delete all tasks

The settings screen follows the same theme and localization rules as the rest of the app.

## 12. Persistence Flow

Storage layers:
- `FlutterSecureStorage` for sensitive credentials
- `SharedPreferences` for settings and app preferences
- Hive for offline task data
- Firestore for cloud sync

Task sync behavior:
- local data is the resilient offline source
- Firestore sync merges local and cloud data using task IDs and timestamps

## 13. Failure / Recovery Notes

- If notifications are disabled, scheduling becomes a no-op.
- If battery optimization is active, notifications may still be delayed by the OS.
- If the device is offline, tasks remain available locally through Hive.
- When the app returns online, repository sync reconciles local and cloud changes.
